 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by ray-triangle intersection tests and neural network inference per pixel. The complexity is $O(N_{rays} \cdot \log N_{faces})$ or $O(N_{rays})$ with a BVH acceleration structure, plus the cost of the neural network evaluation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the mesh size ($O(V + F)$) and the neural network size. The source used an NVIDIA RTX 2080Ti with 11GB GPU memory [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Training times reported are 25-60 minutes for specific datasets on the mentioned hardware [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by ray-triangle intersection tests and neural network inference per pixel [G].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the mesh size ($O(V + F)$) and the neural network size [G].
+*   **Performance:** Training times reported are 25-60 minutes for specific datasets on an NVIDIA RTX 2080Ti GPU with 11GB memory [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Captures geometric intricacies without textures; differentiable rendering allows for end-to-end optimization of mesh and network [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations:** Assumes solid/opaque objects; sensitive to mesh resolution and ray density balance; requires a pre-existing or optimized mesh [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Mesh-based Neural Rendering was proposed as an alternative to implicit neural representations (like NeRF) that use volume rendering. It leverages the efficiency of rasterization or ray-triangle intersection on explicit meshes while retaining the expressive power of neural networks for appearance modeling. The method builds on the principles of [Isosurface and first-hit ray casting](20-isosurface-and-first-hit-ray-casting.md) by using the first hit but replaces the volume integral with a direct neural evaluation at the surface point [S1, unofficial]. It is used in surface reconstruction and novel view synthesis tasks where explicit geometry is desired [S1, unofficial].
